prompt:
The research_archive/ folder on the desktop contains all the materials of a research direction (agent evaluation) that I worked on over the past year: paper PDFs, reading notes (notes/), experiment code documentation, experiment logs (experiment_logs/), Git commit records, report documents for my advisor, and meeting records. This project has been stalled for half a year.
I forget where I got to with this idea back then. Please help me restore the full picture of this research project:
1. Restore the research question as it was then (it may need to be pieced together from the report documents and notes);
2. Identify the hypotheses that have been validated and those that have been refuted;
3. Determine which experimental results are credible based on the experiment logs (note: several sets of experiments had their configurations changed midway, so the results may not be comparable);
4. Reconstruct the specific state at the time of the last work (what I was doing last and where I got stuck);
5. Identify the open questions that were recorded but not resolved at the time;
6. Check the scattered notes I wrote later (during the stalled period) to see whether there are new ideas that can connect to the original research;
7. Finally generate a “research state reconstruction report” saved to /root/Desktop/research_archive/research_state.md, and give the most worthwhile next-step experiment plan (ordered by priority).

